descartes-discourse-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: descartes-discourse-1.logic.r1 has "not_all_mistaken" and "content ∧ not_all_mistaken → reason_equal" and "reason_equal → ¬diversity_from_reason"; descartes-discourse-1.logic.r1.2 and descartes-discourse-1.logic.r1.3 have "not_all_mistaken" and "content ∧ not_all_mistaken → reason_equal"; they differ when only "everyone thinks himself abundantly provided with good sense and desires no larger measure of it", "not all are mistaken in this conviction", "good sense or reason, the power of judging aright and distinguishing truth from error, is by nature equal in all men (good sense is the most equally distributed thing)" and "the diversity of our opinions arises from some being endowed with a larger share of reason than others" are true, which the second allows
descartes-discourse-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps descartes-discourse-1.logic.r1, to: Suppose "everyone thinks himself abundantly provided with good sense and desires no larger measure of it", "not all are mistaken in this conviction", "good sense or reason, the power of judging aright and distinguishing truth from error, is by nature equal in all men (good sense is the most equally distributed thing)" and "the diversity of our opinions arises from some being endowed with a larger share of reason than others" are true. Is S2, "And in this it is not likely that all are mistaken the conviction is rather to be held as testifying that the power of judging aright and of distinguishing truth from error, which is properly what is called good sense or reason, is by nature equal in all men; and that the diversity of our opinions, consequently, does not arise from some being endowed with a larger share of reason than others, but solely from this, that we conduct our thoughts along different ways, and do not fix our attention on the same objects.", true then?
descartes-discourse-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: descartes-discourse-1.logic.r1 has "diversity_from_reason ∨ diversity_from_conduct"; descartes-discourse-1.logic.r1.2 has "reason_equal → ¬diversity_from_reason" and "diversity_from_reason ∨ diversity_from_conduct"; they differ when only "good sense or reason, the power of judging aright and distinguishing truth from error, is by nature equal in all men (good sense is the most equally distributed thing)" and "the diversity of our opinions arises from some being endowed with a larger share of reason than others" are true, which the first allows; descartes-discourse-1.logic.r1.3 has "reason_equal → ¬diversity_from_reason" and "application_decisive → diversity_from_conduct"
